288 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 289 0 R/Alt(A block diagram. At the top, a purple box: the shell, in TypeScript and Svelte 5, which draws every pixel and does no numerics, draws what a service sends the way the service says to draw it, and runs in its own desktop window or in a browser on the same computer. Two double-headed gray arrows connect it to the two services below, labeled as protocol sds/1 over WebSocket on the same computer, with JSON text frames, binary array frames and an exact version match. Left, a green box: the analysis service, in Python, holding the analysis core carried over from Visual Dynamics (spectra, modal fitting, finite elements, specifications, reports, the scripting API), converting a finished run into a project, with no route to a drive. Right, inside a dashed orange outline labeled drive path, a blue box: the control service, in Rust, with its test modules (Random, MIMO Random, Sine, Mixed Mode, Shock, SRS, MIMO Shock, TWR, Explorer, Recorder), every protection, the law host and the run writer; below it the hardware abstraction, which states what a backend can do and what it has proven; below that two backends, the simulator, whose every run is marked SIM, and NI-DAQmx for NI CompactDAQ hardware. A gray arrow labeled writes goes from the control service to a gray box at lower left, the run folder, which is labeled SIM or HARDWARE and converted into a .hforge project, an HDF5 file with a documented layout; a green arrow labeled reads goes from that box up to the analysis service.)/A[<</O/Layout/Placement/Block>>]/K[1]/Pg 834 0 R>>
endobj
